  {
    "source_event_id": "29493",
    "source_url": "https://ibraaz.org/whats-on/who-is-your-we",
    "payload": {
      "categories": [
        {
          "slug": "talks",
          "title": "Talk"
        }
      ],
      "datesText": {
        "html": "<p>Thu 1 Oct, 6–8pm</p>"
      },
      "description": "<p style=\"margin-left:0px;\">Whether it's the family WhatsApp group, a five-a-side football team, a choir, a union, or a work team, our lives are mapped across collective being in ways that ground the every day. But what makes a group a 'we', and what do we owe it?</p><p style=\"margin-left:0px;\">This event takes Ibraaz’s current exhibition <i>Rakyat Pasti Menang</i> from Indonesian collective Taring Padi as a starting point for answering this question. In their 30 years of existence, Taring Padi’s determination to ‘agitate, educate, and organise’ has been structured around a set of principles that allow them to mobilise and exist as a ‘we’.</p><p style=\"margin-left:0px;\">Through other examples of global, local and personal networks of collective being, this event explores how collective realities can be shaped and how we can understand our responsibility to the collectives we belong to.</p><p style=\"margin-left:0px;\"><strong>Doors open at 5.30pm; event starts at 6pm and ends at 8pm.</strong></p><p>Image courtesy of Taring Padi.</p>",
      "endDate": "2026-10-01T20:00:00+00:00",
      "heading": "Hekayyatna and Al Shaibani",
      "id": 29493,
      "image_url": "https://ibraaz-website.imgix.net/29493/RapatTP-1.jpg",
      "location": "Majlis",
      "slug": "who-is-your-we",
      "startDate": "2026-10-01T18:00:00+00:00",
      "title": "Who is your 'we'?",
      "url": "https://ibraaz.org/whats-on/who-is-your-we"
    }
  },
  {
    "source_event_id": "29753",
    "source_url": "https://ibraaz.org/whats-on/colonial-sensitivities",
    "payload": {
      "categories": [
        {
          "slug": "talks",
          "title": "Talk"
        }
      ],
      "datesText": {
        "html": "<p>Sat 3 Oct, 3–4.30pm</p>"
      },
      "description": "<p><strong>Randa Abdel-Fattah</strong>'s <i>Discipline</i> is a searing critique of Western liberalism and its complicity with the enduring colonial violence of Zionism. The novel, first published in Australia in 2025, follows a cast of Arab Muslim characters working in media and educational institutions who are forced to choose between conflict and compromise with the mainstream colonial culture. Those same institutions – the media, literary festivals, higher education establishments – have repeatedly sought to discipline Abdel-Fattah in real life, attempting to silence her through smear campaigns and cancellations. </p><p>Ibraaz welcomes Randa Abdel-Fattah to London to mark the UK launch of <i>Discipline</i>, where she will be in conversation with writer <strong>Omar Robert Hamilton</strong>. </p>",
      "endDate": "2026-10-03T17:00:00+00:00",
      "heading": "Randa Abdel-Fattah in conversation",
      "id": 29753,
      "image_url": "https://ibraaz-website.imgix.net/29753/Randa2.png",
      "location": "Majlis",
      "slug": "colonial-sensitivities",
      "startDate": "2026-10-03T15:00:00+00:00",
      "title": "Colonial Sensitivities",
      "url": "https://ibraaz.org/whats-on/colonial-sensitivities"
    }
  },
